commonMistakes:
  - title: "Using Math.random() for anything that matters"
    description: "Math.random() is fast but not designed to be unpredictable, and its output can sometimes be reconstructed. For prize draws, passwords or anything security-related, use a cryptographic source such as crypto.getRandomValues, as this tool does."
  - title: "Using x mod n on a raw random number"
    description: "Reducing a random number with % n without rejection sampling gives a small bias towards lower values when n does not divide the generator's range evenly. The effect is tiny for small n but real, and easy to avoid."
  - title: "Asking for more unique numbers than the range holds"
    description: "There are only 10 different whole numbers from 1 to 10, so you cannot draw 11 without a repeat. The tool tells you when the count is larger than the range."
  - title: "Expecting random numbers to look evenly spread"
    description: "Real randomness produces streaks and clusters. Rolling three 6s in a row on a fair die happens about once in 216 tries; it is not evidence that the generator is broken."
  - title: "Treating decimals as continuous"
    description: "With 2 decimal places between 0 and 1 there are exactly 101 possible values. If you need finer values, increase the decimal places."
faqs:
  - q: "Is this random number generator truly random?"
    a: "It uses crypto.getRandomValues, a cryptographically secure generator built into your browser and seeded by your operating system's entropy sources. It is suitable for games, draws and sampling, and the numbers cannot be predicted from earlier ones."
  - q: "How do I generate a random number between 1 and 100?"
    a: "Set the minimum to 1, the maximum to 100 and how many to 1, then press Generate. Each number from 1 to 100 has exactly a 1% chance."
  - q: "How do I pick random numbers without repeats?"
    a: "Untick Allow duplicates. Every number in the result will be different, as long as you do not ask for more numbers than the range contains."
  - q: "Can I generate random decimal numbers?"
