    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Why Secure Randomness and Rejection Sampling Matter</h2>
      <p>Computers cannot flip coins, so they use algorithms. Ordinary generators such as JavaScript's Math.random() produce numbers that look random but follow a hidden pattern. Cryptographically secure generators, exposed in browsers as crypto.getRandomValues, are seeded from unpredictable events gathered by the operating system and are designed so that seeing earlier outputs does not help predict the next one.</p>
      <p>Getting a number in a range also needs care. A secure generator returns values from 0 to 4,294,967,295. To get a number from 1 to 6 you might take the remainder after dividing by 6, but 4,294,967,296 is not a multiple of 6, so the remainders 0 to 3 would come up very slightly more often than 4 and 5. This tool throws away the handful of raw values that cause that imbalance and draws again, so every face is exactly equally likely.</p>
